NAME
====

**tenter** - run commands in response to signed GitHub webhooks

SYNOPSIS
========

**tenter** [**-H** _address_ | **--host** _address_] [**-p** _port_ | **--port** _port_]
       [**-r** _dir_ | **--doc-root** _dir_] [**--config-filename** _name_]
       [**--command-dir** _name_] [**--log-file** _path_] [**--no-log**]
       [**--no-timestamp**]

**tenter** [**-v** | **--version**]

**tenter** [**-h** | **--help**]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**tenter** is an HTTP server that runs a command when it receives a POST
request signed with a secret. It is intended to receive webhooks from
GitHub.

Each exposed directory is a directory under the document root:

````
<doc-root>/<directory>/
├── hooks.edn           the directory's secret
├── commands/<command>  each command that can be run
└── log/commands.log    the output of each run
````

A POST request to ``/run/<command>/in/<directory>`` (with or without a
trailing ``/``) runs ``<doc-root>/<directory>/commands/<command>`` if the
request's ``X-Hub-Signature-256`` header is the HMAC-SHA256 of its body
under the directory's secret, as GitHub sends it. The response is sent once
the command has started; **tenter** does not wait for the command to
finish.

**tenter** runs until it is stopped. It prints the address it is listening
on, then the request line and status of each request to standard output, and
the reason for each refused request to standard error.

OPTIONS
=======

- **-H** _address_ | **--host** _address_:
  The address to listen on. This defaults to ``127.0.0.1``. **tenter** does
  not use TLS, so it prints a warning if this is not a loopback address. See
  `<SECURITY>`.

- **-p** _port_ | **--port** _port_:
  The port to listen on. This defaults to ``4567``. With ``0``, a free port is
  chosen and printed.

- **-r** _dir_ | **--doc-root** _dir_:
  The directory that holds each exposed directory. It must be an absolute
  path. This defaults to ``/var/www``.

- **--config-filename** _name_:
  The name of the configuration file in each exposed directory. This
  defaults to ``hooks.edn``.

- **--command-dir** _name_:
  The name of the directory of commands in each exposed directory. This
  defaults to ``commands``.

- **--log-file** _path_:
  The path of the log in each exposed directory, relative to it. It cannot
  contain ``.`` or ``..``. This defaults to ``log/commands.log``. The log's
  directory must exist.

- **--no-log**:
  Discard the output of commands.

- **--no-timestamp**:
  Do not begin each line of the log with the time.

- **-v** | **--version**:
  Print the version and exit.

- **-h** | **--help**:
  Print a help message and exit.

CONFIGURATION
=============

The configuration file of an exposed directory is one EDN map with a
``:secret``, a non-empty string:

````
{:secret "a long random string"}
````

The file is read as data; nothing in it is evaluated. It is read on each
request, so a change takes effect without restarting **tenter**. A file that
is missing, is not a map, has a key other than ``:secret`` or has an empty
secret refuses every request for its directory.

The secret is the one entered in the webhook's settings on GitHub. The
webhook's content type does not matter, since the signature covers the body
as it is sent.

COMMANDS
========

A command must be an executable file directly in the command directory. A
symlink in its place is refused, as is a command directory that resolves to a
path outside its exposed directory. Directory and command names in the URL
can contain letters, digits, ``.``, ``_`` and ``-``, and must begin with a
letter, digit or ``_``.

A command is run directly, not through a shell, with no arguments. Its
working directory is the command directory and its standard input is
``/dev/null``. Its environment has ``PATH``, ``HOME``, ``LANG``, ``LC_ALL``
and ``TZ`` from **tenter**'s environment (where set), ``TENTER_DIRECTORY``,
the name of the exposed directory, and ``TENTER_COMMAND``, the name of the
command. Nothing else in **tenter**'s environment is passed on.

Only one run of a command is in progress at a time. A request that arrives
during a run is answered with ``Command queued``, and the command runs once
more when the run ends, however many requests arrived. Different commands
run at the same time.

LOG
===

The standard output and standard error of each run are appended to the log as
the command writes them. A run begins with an ``Initiating:`` line and ends
with a ``Finished:`` line that gives the exit status, or a ``Failed:`` line
if the command could not be started (for example, a script without a ``#!``
line). Each line begins with the local time:

````
[2026-11-20 11:47:38 +0900] Initiating: /var/www/example/commands/rebuild
[2026-11-20 11:47:38 +0900] Building...
[2026-11-20 11:47:41 +0900] Finished: /var/www/example/commands/rebuild (exit status 0)
````

If the log cannot be opened, the command is not run.

RESPONSES
=========

Each response is plain text and closes the connection.

- **200** ``Command initiated`` or ``Command queued``.
- **400** ``X-Hub-Signature-256 header not set``, ``Command not found`` or
  ``Bad request``.
- **403** ``X-Hub-Signature-256 header did not match``. This is the response
  to a wrong signature, to an unknown directory and to a directory with a
  configuration file that cannot be used, so that the response does not
  show which.
- **404** for any other path and **405** for any method but POST.
- **408** if the request is not sent within 10 seconds.
- **411** without ``Content-Length``, **413** for a body over 1 MiB, **431**
  for headers over 16 KiB and **501** with ``Transfer-Encoding``.
- **500** if the log cannot be opened.
- **503** when 32 connections are already being handled.

SECURITY
========

**tenter** runs programs for anyone who has a directory's secret. Use a long
random secret, and keep the configuration file readable only by the user
that runs **tenter**.

**tenter** does not use TLS. Run it on a loopback address behind a reverse
proxy, such as nginx, that provides HTTPS. Without TLS, the secret is not
sent, but the payload can be read in transit and a captured request can be
sent again. A signature covers only the body, so a request can be sent again
in any case; it runs the command again.

Run **tenter** as an unprivileged user, not as root. Commands run as the same
user.

Once it is listening, **tenter** gives up the capabilities it does not need,
including compiling code, reading the environment and making outbound
connections.

EXAMPLES
========

Serve the directories in ``/srv/www`` on port 9000:

````
$ tenter -r /srv/www -p 9000
Listening on http://127.0.0.1:9000/
````

Send a signed request with ``curl`` and ``openssl``:

````
$ body='{}'
$ sig=$(printf '%s' "$body" | openssl dgst -sha256 -hmac "$SECRET" | sed 's/.* //')
$ curl -X POST -H "X-Hub-Signature-256: sha256=$sig" --data "$body" \
    http://127.0.0.1:9000/run/rebuild/in/example/
Command initiated
````

EXIT STATUS
===========

**tenter** exits with 1 if its options are invalid or it cannot listen. It
otherwise runs until it is stopped.
